  "success": true,
  "output": "Next 16 / React 19, confirmed. Here's the analysis.\n\n## Codebase state — read this first\n\nBefore the findings: most of this repo is intentional scaffolding, not incomplete code. Per `AGENTS.md`'s build order, everything in `engine/pipeline/`, `engine/connectors/salesforce.py`, `engine/connectors/warehouse.py`, `engine/agent/graph.py` (except `should_auto_apply`), `engine/agent/prompts.py`, `engine/db.py`, `engine/main.py:execute_run`, and all of `engine/eval/` is `raise NotImplementedError(...)`. The web layer has a landing page, get-started page, a read-only sources page, and Clerk auth — no runs, no conflict queue, no client-side state at all (zero `useState`/`useEffect`/`useMemo`/`useCallback` in the whole `web/` tree).\n\nSo: **there are no N+1 queries, no re-render bugs, and no memory leaks to find in running code, because the reconciliation pipeline, the connectors, and the conflict queue don't execute yet.** Reporting fabricated findings against stub functions would be noise. Instead, below is (1) the one real, fixable issue that exists today, and (2) concrete, code-grounded risks in the *signatures and docstrings already committed* — these are the places the design will go wrong or right once Phase 2–5 land, called out against the actual file/line so they're actionable when you implement them, not generic advice.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Redundant computation — real, fixable now\n\n`web/lib/server/sources.ts:23-30`\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();   // ← full SELECT + Zod-parse of every row\n  return {\n    crm: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\").length,\n    warehouse: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"databricks\" || s.kind === \"snowflake\").length,\n    synthetic: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"synthetic\").length,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nNot an N+1 today (nothing calls both functions on the same page yet), but it's a duplicate-round-trip trap: the moment `sources/page.tsx` wants both the table *and* the counts (e.g. a \"3 CRM · 2 warehouse\" header), you'll pay two DB round trips plus two full Zod-parse passes for data one query already had. Two fixes, cheapest first:\n\n```ts\n// Option A — dedupe within a single render pass with React's cache()\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\n\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n});\n```\n`cache()` memoizes per-request, so `listSources()` called from both the page and `countSourcesBySide()` in the same render hits Postgres once.\n\n```ts\n// Option B — push the count to SQL if you need it independent of the row set\nimport { sql } from \"drizzle-orm\";\n\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb()\n    .select({ kind: sources.kind, n: sql<number>`count(*)::int` })\n    .from(sources)\n    .groupBy(sources.kind);\n  // fold rows into { crm, warehouse, synthetic }\n}\n```\n\n## 2. Caching — one real win, one already-correct pattern to keep\n\n- **Real win:** the `cache()` wrap above. Apply it now, before `sources/page.tsx` and a future `get-started` header both need the data.\n- **Already correct, don't touch:** `web/lib/db/index.ts:22-44` memoizes the Postgres client on `globalThis` specifically to survive Next's dev-mode HMR reloads, and lazily connects so `next build` doesn't require a live DB. That's the right pattern — flagging it so it isn't \"fixed\" into something worse later.\n- **Forward, Phase 3:** `engine/agent/prompts.py:27-34` already separates `build_ruleset_block` (identical per run) from `build_conflict_block` (varies per conflict) — that split is exactly what prompt caching needs. When you implement the Anthropic call, the cache breakpoint has to go on the ruleset block specifically:\n  ```python\n  messages = [{\n      \"role\": \"user\",\n      \"content\": [\n          {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": ruleset_block, \"cache_control\": {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}},\n          {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": conflict_block},\n      ],\n  }]\n  ```\n  Get this wrong (e.g. concatenate ruleset+conflict into one string before caching) and the cache never hits, because the per-conflict suffix changes the prefix hash every call — silently paying full price on every one of the \"resolve conflicts by the thousand\" calls the eval harness is meant to sweep.\n- **Forward, Phase 4:** no run-status polling exists yet, but `AGENTS.md` reserves TanStack Query for exactly that. When it lands, gate `refetchInterval` on run status so a completed run stops polling:\n  ```ts\n  useQuery({\n    queryKey: [\"run\", runId, \"status\"],\n    queryFn: () => fetchRunStatus(runId),\n    refetchInterval: (query) =>\n      query.state.data?.status === \"complete\" || query.state.data?.status === \"failed\"\n        ? false\n        : 2000,\n  });\n  ```\n\n## 3. Memory leaks — nothing live yet; two design risks worth flagging before you build them\n\n- `engine/main.py:38` — `background.add_task(execute_run, body.run_id)`. FastAPI `BackgroundTasks` run in-process, on the same event loop, with no queue or concurrency cap. `execute_run` is specified to do embedding + FAISS + LangGraph work — CPU-bound and slow. Two runs kicked off close together will pile up in one process instead of queuing, and a slow run blocks the `/status` polling endpoint for everyone. Worth deciding now whether that's acceptable for a single-tenant tool or whether you want a bounded worker (e.g. a simple semaphore, or a real queue) before Phase 1 ships — flagging for your call, not adding a dependency without asking per `AGENTS.md`.\n- `engine/pipeline/blocking.py` (Phase 2, not yet implemented): `sentence-transformers` model loads are expensive (seconds, hundreds of MB). If `block()` instantiates the model inside the function instead of at module scope, every run reloads it. Load once as a module-level singleton, same pattern as `lib/db/index.ts`'s cached client:\n  ```python\n  _model: SentenceTransformer | None = None\n\n  def _get_model() -> SentenceTransformer:\n      global _model\n      if _model is None:\n          _model = SentenceTransformer(\"all-MiniLM-L6-v2\")\n      return _model\n  ```\n- `engine/db.py` (Phase 1/2): every function here (`write_entities`, `write_matches`, `write_conflicts`, `write_resolutions`, `append_audit`) needs a Postgres connection. If each opens its own `psycopg.connect(...)`, a single run leaks connections until they're exhausted — pool it once, module-level, mirroring the exact caching discipline already used in `web/lib/db/index.ts`.\n\n## 4. N+1 queries — none exist; the signatures already committed guard against the obvious trap\n\n`entities: dict[UUID, CanonicalEntity]` as the parameter to `detect()` in `engine/pipeline/detect.py:38-43` is the right shape — a lookup dict, not a per-match DB fetch. Keep that when you implement it: resolve every entity for a run once into that dict before the diff loop, don't query per match.\n\nThe one thing to watch when `engine/agent/graph.py:resolve()` (currently `raise NotImplementedError`, line 64-69) gets built: it takes the full `rules: list[SurvivorshipRule]` and a batch of conflicts. `select_rule` needs \"most specific first, first match wins\" ordering — if that means re-sorting or re-filtering `rules` inside the loop for every conflict in a batch of thousands, that's an O(n·m) redundant-computation trap disguised as a query problem. Sort/index the ruleset once, outside the loop:\n\n```python\ndef resolve(conflicts: list[Conflict], rules: list[SurvivorshipRule]) -> list[Resolution]:\n    ranked_rules = sorted(rules, key=_specificity_key, reverse=True)  # once\n    return [_resolve_one(c, ranked_rules) for c in conflicts]          # no re-sort per conflict\n```\n\n## 5. Unnecessary React re-renders — none possible today; the console architecture is set up to avoid the coming one\n\nNo component holds state, so there's nothing to re-render unnecessarily right now. `NavRail` (`web/components/console/nav-rail.tsx`) is the only component using a reactive hook (`usePathname`), and it's correctly scoped: it's its own client leaf inside a Server Component layout (`(console)/layout.tsx`), so route changes re-render the rail, not the page content next to it. Keep that boundary discipline for Phase 4.\n\nThe place it'll matter is the conflict queue — `AGENTS.md`'s own spec calls for \"a queue of 400 conflicts,\" `j`/`k` keyboard nav, and severity filtering. When that's built:\n- Keep filter/severity state in a client-component *island* around the queue, not lifted to the page or layout — otherwise every keystroke re-renders the nav rail and header too.\n- Memoize the filtered/sorted list (`useMemo`) so filtering doesn't re-run on every keystroke of an unrelated field, and wrap each `ConflictDiffRow` in `React.memo` so approving/rejecting one row doesn't re-render the other 399.\n- At 400 rows with the stagger-in animation `AGENTS.md` specifies, consider windowing (e.g. render only the visible rows) once the list is real — the spec explicitly says the animation should run once on arrival, not on every scroll, so don't accidentally re-trigger `motion.li`'s `initial` state on re-render (keep `key` stable per conflict id, not per array index).\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line:** one concrete fix available today (`sources.ts` — 5 minutes), and the rest is a punch list to check against as you implement Phases 2–5, since that's when N+1s, re-renders, and leaks actually become possible. Happy to re-run this same analysis once `blocking.py`/`detect.py`/`graph.py` have real bodies — that's where the interesting perf work will actually live.\n",
